## 3. Data Model and Storage Architecture

### Storage zones

Four zones with strict boundaries enforced by the Swift module import graph:

1. **Device — encrypted local store.** Standard SwiftData/CoreData with file protection. Most app data lives here.
2. **Device-sealed — separate store, AI layer cannot import directly.** Period tracking, sensitive memory, photo bytes. The `OHTTPProvider` and any third-party-bound module physically cannot import these types. On-device AI may access **abstracted signals derived from sealed data** via the `PeriodContextBridge` module, but never the raw entries.
3. **Cloud — minimal.** Friend links, fuzzy state, ephemeral hearts, activity rosters. Public keys and short-lived metadata only. No content, no personal data, no health metrics.
4. **Transient — inference context.** Exists only for the duration of a Foundation Models or CoreML inference call. Apple's Foundation Models framework is stateless: each call receives input, produces output, and the context window is discarded. No on-device fine-tuning occurs, no data persists in the model between calls, and no training data is retained. CoreML classifiers are similarly inference-only. The only persistence is what the app code explicitly writes to its own stores.

### Core types

#### User *(device)*
`id, displayName, goalType, macroGoals {protein, carbs, fat}, createdAt, aiProvidersEnabled {perFeatureTier}`

Visible appearance lives in `UserCustomization`, not on `User`.

#### MealDefinition *(device)* — reusable meal template
`id, name, sourceType (photo | recipe | restaurant | manual), macros {protein, carbs, fat}, qualitySignal {variety, healthiness, sufficiency}, ingredients (optional, for recipes), sourceMetadata (chain id, recipe text, or photo ref), createdAt, lastUsedAt, useCount, userCorrected`

#### MealLog *(device)* — timestamped "I ate this" record
`id, definitionId → MealDefinition, timestamp, servings (multiplier, default 1.0), notes (optional)`

Logging a repeat meal = `new MealLog` pointing at an existing `MealDefinition`. No re-parsing, no re-searching.

#### Workout *(device)*
`id, timestamp, type, exercises [{name, sets, reps, weight}], durationMin, healthKitRef, healthKitWorkoutId (nullable — set when written back to HealthKit), heartRateAvg, activeEnergy, source (fernlet | healthKit | both)`

The `source` field tracks whether a workout was logged in-app, imported from HealthKit, or reconciled from both. See [Section 4](#4-healthkit-and-sensor-integration) for the bidirectional sync.

#### JournalEntry *(device)*
`id, date, colorTag, text (≤100 words), derivedEmotions [keys], attachedPhotoId (nullable → Photo)`

The `derivedEmotions` field is the abstracted form the AI layer is allowed to see; raw `text` is never included in AI context by default. The ~100-word limit (~500–700 characters) balances expressiveness with low-friction journaling — enough to capture a real thought, short enough that opening the journal never feels like a writing assignment.

