## 4. HealthKit and Sensor Integration

### HealthKit reads

The app requests read authorization for these HealthKit data types via `HKHealthStore.requestAuthorization()`:

- `HKQuantityTypeIdentifier.heartRate` — for workout heart rate averages and intensity inference
- `HKQuantityTypeIdentifier.activeEnergyBurned` — for movement baseline detection and exercise scoring
- `HKCategoryTypeIdentifier.sleepAnalysis` — for sleep stage data (iOS 16+)
- `HKQuantityTypeIdentifier.stepCount` — for the movement baseline floor
- `HKWorkoutType.workoutType()` — for importing workouts logged on Apple Watch or other apps

`NSHealthShareUsageDescription` in Info.plist explains why each is needed in plain language.

### HealthKit write-back

Fernlet writes workouts back to HealthKit so they appear in Apple Health, contribute to Apple Watch activity rings, and are visible to any other app the user has authorized. This is critical for users who rely on Apple Health as their central health dashboard — workouts logged in Fernlet shouldn't be invisible to the rest of the ecosystem.

**What is written:**
- `HKWorkout` samples for each Fernlet-logged workout, including type, duration, active energy (if available from the session), and start/end timestamps
- `HKQuantityType.activeEnergyBurned` samples associated with the workout, if Fernlet has enough data to estimate (from HealthKit heart rate during the workout window)

**What is NOT written:**
- No sleep data (Fernlet reads sleep, doesn't generate it)
- No hydration data (HealthKit's hydration type exists but isn't widely used and would create confusing duplicates)
- No journal, mood, or hygiene data (no corresponding HealthKit types)
- No period data (Fernlet's period tracker is intentionally separate from HealthKit's menstrual cycle data types to maintain the `PrivateHealthStore` wall)

**Write authorization:** Requires `NSHealthUpdateUsageDescription` ("Fernlet writes your logged workouts to Apple Health so they count toward your activity rings and are visible in other health apps.") and a separate `HKHealthStore.requestAuthorization(toShare:)` call. Users can deny write permission while still allowing reads — workouts just won't appear in Apple Health.

### Bidirectional workout sync

**Fernlet → HealthKit:** When the user logs a workout in Fernlet, the app writes it to HealthKit and stores the resulting `HKWorkout` UUID in `Workout.healthKitWorkoutId` to prevent duplicates.

**HealthKit → Fernlet:** When the user opens the Exercise screen or when a daily score is computed, Fernlet queries HealthKit for recent `HKWorkout` samples not already linked to a Fernlet `Workout` record. New external workouts (from Apple Watch, Peloton, Strava, etc.) are imported as `Workout` records with `source: .healthKit`, using the workout type, duration, active energy, and heart rate data from HealthKit. These imported workouts contribute to the exercise component score just like manually logged ones.

**Deduplication:** The `healthKitWorkoutId` field and HealthKit's `HKSource` metadata prevent double-counting. A workout logged in Fernlet and written to HealthKit will not be re-imported as a separate entry. A workout logged on Apple Watch and imported into Fernlet will not be re-written to HealthKit.

**User control:** A toggle in Settings → HealthKit controls write-back. Default is on (if write permission is granted). Users who prefer Fernlet to be read-only can disable write-back without losing any in-app functionality.

### Sleep quality derivation

HealthKit does not provide a native sleep quality score. Fernlet derives one from the raw sleep stage data using a defined formula:

```
qualityScore = (
  0.40 × (totalAsleepHours / ageTargetHours, capped at 1.0) +
  0.25 × (deepSleepRatio normalized to typical 13–23% range) +
  0.20 × (remSleepRatio normalized to typical 20–25% range) +
  0.15 × (1 - awakeningsPenalty)
)

awakeningsPenalty = min(awakeCount / 6, 1.0)
```

If sleep stage data is unavailable (e.g., user doesn't wear an Apple Watch to bed and only has time-in-bed from iPhone autodetect), the score falls back to:

```
qualityScore = (totalSleepHours / ageTargetHours), capped at 1.0
```

The presence of stage data is recorded in `SleepRecord.source` so the UI can subtly indicate "richer data available with Apple Watch" without nagging.

### WeatherKit

Used for the Gentle Mood-Recovery Prompts feature ([Section 11](#11-ambient-features)). WeatherKit is included in the Apple Developer Program at 500,000 calls per month free, with paid tiers for higher volume. Fernlet's expected call volume (one weather check per mood-recovery prompt evaluation, rate-limited per user) is well under the free tier.

WeatherKit requires only coordinates (no user identifier), and Apple states location data is not associated with personal information or tracked between requests. Coarse location (city-level) is sufficient for the use case.

