## 15. Privacy Architecture and Data Flow

### What stays on-device

- All raw journal text
- All sensitive memory entries
- All period entries, predictions, and health trends
- All photo bytes
- All AI inferences when using the on-device tier
- All scoring computation
- All meal definitions and logs
- All workout logs (also written to HealthKit if user permits)
- Background scene composition
- User-created clothing designs (until in-person shared)

### What syncs via iCloud Keychain

- Identity keypairs (Ed25519 + X25519). End-to-end encrypted.

### What goes to HealthKit (local, user-controlled)

- Workout samples (written back so they count toward Apple Watch rings and appear in Apple Health)
- No period data, no mood data, no journal data, no hydration data

### What goes to the cloud (minimal, public-key-keyed only)

- `Friend` (id, displayName, publicKey, fuzzyState, lastSeenActive)
- `FriendLink` (userA, userB, establishedAt, establishedVia)
- `Activity` (id, creatorId, title, type, startedAt, participants, optional coarse location)
- `ActivityJoinToken` (ephemeral)
- `HeartSent` (ephemeral, ~24h)

No content, no health metrics, no scores, no journal data, no photos, no period data, no memory.

### What goes to third-party AI providers (explicit per-feature opt-in only)

See [Section 16](#16-third-party-integrations-and-cloud-fallbacks). Routed through OHTTP. Payloads constructed by `ContextBuilder` with strict allowlists. `OHTTPProvider` **cannot import** `PeriodEntry`, `PeriodContextBridge`, `SensitiveMemory`, or `Photo` — compile-time enforced. Period-aware workout and meal adjustments are only available when using the on-device AI tier.

